6f3e KDV decode dispatch: the chunk buffer [0x6e80] is dword-indexed here -- restore explicit
4-byte field accesses on the byte-addressed pointer.

FUN_0000_6f3e reads a KDV chunk (via 708b), then dispatches on the 4-byte MAGIC at the chunk buffer
[0x6e80] and reads dword header fields.  In FLAT32 the pointer-valued DAT_0000_6e80 is a BYTE pointer
(so the read path 708b's byte offsets [ebx+4]/[ebx+8] and the INT-21 buffer shadows are correct --
see the assembler's undefined1** ptrvar rule).  6f3e, by contrast, accesses the SAME buffer with
DWORD granularity, which Ghidra rendered as int* derefs/indices (`*DAT_6e80`, `DAT_6e80[2..4]`,
`DAT_6e80 + 2`).  On a byte pointer those become 1-byte / +2..+4-byte accesses -> the magic compare
reads one byte (0x78) instead of 0x4b445678, no chunk type ever matches, and the player loops reading
without ever decoding/presenting a frame.  asm-verified (image 0x6f4b..0x6fac):
    6f51: mov eax,[ebx]        ; magic dword           @ +0
    6f5a: mov eax,[ebx+0x8]    ; width  dword          @ +8
    6f62: mov eax,[ebx+0xc]    ; height dword          @ +0xc
    6f6a: mov eax,[ebx+0x10]   ; frame-count dword     @ +0x10
    6f7b: lea esi,[ebx+8]      ; palette source        @ +8  (rep movs 0xC0 dwords)
    6fa1: add ebx,8            ; image RLE source      @ +8
Restore each as an explicit `*(int *)(DAT_6e80 + <byte off>)` / `(int *)(DAT_6e80 + 8)`.
